<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Black Loyalists</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Black Loyalists were people of African descent, many formerly enslaved, who sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a> in return for promises of freedom. About 3,000 were evacuated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a> in 1783, settling at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birchtown">Birchtown</a> and elsewhere, but faced poor land and discrimination. In 1792 about 1,100 of them sailed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a> and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freetown">Freetown</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/black-loyalists.jpg" alt="Black Loyalists" width="320" height="253" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Di (they-them) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black_Loyalist_Heritage_Society_coat_of_arms.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>As tensions grew before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolution">American Revolution</a>, many enslaved Americans came to believe that King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a> was on their side. On 7 November 1775 Lord Dunmore, the royal governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>, declared free the servants and enslaved people of rebels who were able and willing to bear arms for the Crown. Within a month about 800 had fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norfolk">Norfolk</a> to enlist, though Virginia's enslavers decreed death for runaways. In 1779 Sir Henry Clinton's Philipsburg Proclamation promised freedom to anyone enslaved by a Patriot, though Clinton often returned runaways to Loyalist owners.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Dunmore formed 800 volunteers into the Royal Ethiopian Regiment, which fought at Kemp's Landing and was defeated at the Battle of Great Bridge; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> then spread on the crowded ships, and only 300 survived. The largest unit, the Black Company of Pioneers, built fortifications for Clinton in North Carolina, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, Newport and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a> the small Black Brigade of Colonel Tye, who had escaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in Monmouth County, raided Patriots from 1778 until Tye died of gangrene in 1780, after which Stephen Blucke led it.</p><p>After the siege of Yorktown, Lieutenant General Guy Carleton kept the promise of freedom despite American anger: anyone who could prove service or status received a certificate of freedom and passage to a British colony, and was listed in the Book of Negroes, while enslavers were to be compensated. Others went to Upper Canada, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>. In the confusion of the evacuations from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> and Charleston, American enslavers seized many, even people born free, and about 300 who stayed near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savannah">Savannah</a> were mostly re-enslaved by 1786.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a> they settled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birchtown">Birchtown</a>, Digby, Guysborough County, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annapolis-royal">Annapolis Royal</a>, Preston and Halifax, and Birchtown was for its first years the largest free African community in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>. By 1785 most communities had independent Black churches and many their own schools, but Crown officials gave them rockier, less fertile land than White Loyalists, and in July 1784 they were attacked in the Shelburne Riots, the first recorded race riots in Canadian history. Most of their teachers, preachers and leaders later left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a>, where their descendants are among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone-creole-people">Sierra Leone Creole people</a>, or Krios, whose language drew on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gullah">Gullah</a> and African American Vernacular English. Birchtown became a National Historic Site in 1997.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Black Loyalist” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After 1783 tens of thousands of Loyalists arrived, including <b>Black Loyalists</b> at Birchtown, and New Brunswick was split off in 1784.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-nova-scotia">Colony of Nova Scotia</a></li><li><blockquote>After the failure of the Province of Freedom, the Sierra Leone Company brought about 1,100 <b>Black Loyalists</b> from Nova Scotia under John Clarkson, who founded Freetown in 1792.</blockquote><a href="/state/sierra-leone-colony-and-protectorate">Sierra Leone Colony and Protectorate</a></li></ul></section>
